  16}},ExpressionUUID->"b9c89fbd-4420-41ac-884a-95b3b8dbb560"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The law of innovation \[Dash] a cautionary tale", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"33d26185-6e65-430d-808d-7a662c1dde2c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The published law says ",
 StyleBox["more polysemous words change faster",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". In this corpus the ",
 StyleBox["raw",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " correlation comes out the wrong way: change-",
 "rate vs. polysemy has Spearman \[Rho] = ",
 "-0.31",
 " \[Dash] negative. The culprit is a ",
 StyleBox["confound",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ": polysemous words tend to ",
 "be frequent (\[Rho] = ",
 "0.34",
 "), and frequency strongly suppresses change. In the raw comparison the \
frequency ",
 "effect simply dominates \[Dash] a textbook Simpson's paradox."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"37d9b6c8-c520-4467-9e87-c34eb2598ab6"],

Cell["\<\
Controlling for frequency (partial Spearman correlation, holding frequency \
rank fixed) the spurious negative trend vanishes \[Dash] but it does not turn \
convincingly positive either: partial \[Rho] = 0.01, essentially zero. So in \
the Google-N-grams eng-all corpus, with WordNet sense counts as the polysemy \
measure, the law of innovation does not replicate. That is a more honest \
\[Dash] and more useful \[Dash] result than forcing the sign: the law may \
need a different corpus, a better polysemy measure, or the original \
\>", "authors' frequency-and-polysemy-matched controls to surface.", \
"Text",ExpressionUUID->"b89e9c5a-e070-49b1-9527-2f8b3ab9a08d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 CellMargins -> {{50, 50}, {6, 6}}], "Input",
 CellLabel->" In[2]:=",ExpressionUUID->"058f4c11-dee5-4f0c-90db-ddde13c98840"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure. ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "The raw polysemy trend (blue) slopes the \"wrong\" way only because ",
 "polysemous words are frequent; the frequency-controlled residual (red) is \
flat. Raw ",
 "Spearman ",
 "-0.31",
 ", partial ",
 "0.01",
 "."
}], "Caption",
 CellMargins->{{55, 55}, {2, 
  16}},ExpressionUUID->"cc2313f4-a92e-4722-9d04-7879c9c3aa41"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Takeaway.  ",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "Conformity replicates cleanly; innovation does not survive a frequency ",
 "control here. A large raw correlation is easy to get and easy to misread \
\[Dash] ",
 "always ask what else moves with your variable before naming an effect."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{3, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {8, 12}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.35, 0.6, 0.4],
 FontSize->12,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.93, 0.97, 0.93],ExpressionUUID->"6242fead-dcae-408c-89d7-88903d94dd7c"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8. The most-drifted words \[Dash] and a caveat", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e7435c00-08f3-484e-a907-549d934e2964"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Ranking every stable content word by how far it moved between its 1800s and \
",
 "1990s aligned vectors surfaces two phenomena at once. Some entries are \
genuine ",
 "meaning change (",
 StyleBox["advert",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " verb \[Rule] noun, ",
 StyleBox["peer",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " noble \[Rule] equal, ",
 StyleBox["media",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["broadcast",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "); others are lexical obsolescence or register shift, where an archaic word \
(",
 StyleBox["twelvemonth, fourthly, civilities",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ") simply stopped occurring in the same ",
 "kinds of text. A large embedding displacement means ",
 "\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the contexts changed\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \[Dash] \
which is ",
 "necessary, but not sufficient, for \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]the meaning ",
 "changed\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. Distinguishing the two is where embeddings \
end and ",
 "lexicography begins."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"83f9d150-09b2-4a12-b61b-e43af4aa7739"],

